window.HSC_PHYSICS = {
  name: "Physics", tag: "Class 12 · Maharashtra HSC", marks: 70,
  blurb: "Master the derivations and solve numericals by writing the formula first, then substituting SI units; theory marks are the easiest to secure.",
  units: [
    {
      t: "Rotational Dynamics",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter extends Newtonian mechanics to rotating bodies using moment of inertia, torque and angular momentum. The key idea is that I plays the role of mass in rotation and that angular momentum is conserved when net external torque is zero. Circular motion concepts like centripetal force and banking of roads are also central.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Moment of inertia and radius of gyration", e: "Moment of inertia measures a body's resistance to angular acceleration; radius of gyration is the distance at which the whole mass could be concentrated to give the same I." },
        { t: "Theorems of parallel and perpendicular axes", e: "These theorems let you find the moment of inertia about any axis by relating it to the moment of inertia about a parallel axis through the centre of mass, or about perpendicular axes in a plane." },
        { t: "Angular momentum and its conservation", e: "Angular momentum L = I*w is conserved when no net external torque acts, which explains why a rotating body speeds up when its moment of inertia decreases." },
        { t: "Rolling motion and kinetic energy", e: "A rolling body has both translational and rotational kinetic energy linked by the rolling condition v = R*w, giving total KE = (1/2)mv^2[1 + k^2/R^2]." },
        { t: "Banking of roads and conical pendulum", e: "Banking a road tilts its surface so the horizontal component of the normal force supplies the centripetal force needed for safe turning at a given speed, similar to the geometry of a conical pendulum." },
        { t: "Torque and rotational equivalent of Newton's law", e: "Torque tau = I*alpha is the rotational analogue of F = ma, where moment of inertia replaces mass and angular acceleration replaces linear acceleration." }
      ],
      core: "Derivations of M.I. expressions and conservation of angular momentum; banking of road formula numericals.",
      prep: "Memorize the M.I. table for standard bodies and practice applying parallel/perpendicular axis theorems; drill vmax on a banked curve and rolling KE split numericals.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A body of M.I. 3 kg m^2 rotating at 2 rad/s is acted on by a torque that increases its speed to 10 rad/s in 4 s. Find the applied torque.", a: "Angular acceleration alpha = (w2 - w1)/t = (10 - 2)/4 = 2 rad/s^2; Torque tau = I*alpha = 3 x 2 = 6 N m; Final answer: 6 N m." },
        { q: "State and derive the expression for kinetic energy of a rolling body and define rolling condition.", a: "A rolling body has both translational and rotational KE; Total KE = (1/2)Mv^2 + (1/2)I*w^2; With rolling condition v = R*w and I = M*k^2, I*w^2 = M*k^2*(v^2/R^2); So KE = (1/2)Mv^2 + (1/2)M*k^2*v^2/R^2 = (1/2)Mv^2[1 + k^2/R^2]; This is the rolling KE where k is radius of gyration." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Mechanical Properties of Fluids",
      wt: "~4 marks",
      explain: "This chapter studies fluids at rest and in motion, covering pressure, surface tension, viscosity and fluid flow. The key ideas are that surface tension arises from molecular cohesion, viscosity opposes relative motion of layers, and Bernoulli's principle relates pressure, speed and height along a streamline. Capillary rise and terminal velocity are important applications.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Surface tension and surface energy", e: "Surface tension is the force per unit length acting along a liquid surface due to molecular cohesion, and surface energy is the work needed to increase surface area against this force." },
        { t: "Angle of contact and capillarity", e: "The angle of contact between a liquid and solid surface determines whether a liquid wets the surface and rises or falls in a narrow capillary tube." },
        { t: "Viscosity and Stokes' law", e: "Viscosity is the internal friction between fluid layers moving at different speeds, and Stokes' law gives the viscous drag force F = 6*pi*eta*r*v on a small sphere moving through a fluid." },
        { t: "Terminal velocity", e: "Terminal velocity is the constant maximum speed a falling body reaches in a viscous fluid when the viscous drag plus buoyancy balance the weight." },
        { t: "Equation of continuity and Bernoulli's principle", e: "The equation of continuity (A*v = constant) expresses conservation of mass in flow, while Bernoulli's principle extends this with energy conservation relating pressure, speed and height." },
        { t: "Reynolds number and types of flow", e: "Reynolds number is a dimensionless ratio of inertial to viscous forces that predicts whether flow will be smooth (laminar) or chaotic (turbulent)." }
      ],
      core: "Capillary rise derivation, Stokes' law and terminal velocity, Bernoulli's equation and its applications.",
      prep: "Learn the capillary rise and terminal velocity formulas and watch units of surface tension (N/m) and viscosity (Pa s); practice Bernoulli numericals with venturimeter.",
      questions: [
        { q: "Water rises to a height of 5 cm in a capillary tube of radius 0.2 mm. Find the surface tension of water. (g = 9.8 m/s^2, density = 1000 kg/m^3, angle of contact = 0).", a: "Formula: h = 2T*cos(theta)/(r*rho*g), so T = h*r*rho*g/(2cos(theta)); Substitute h = 0.05 m, r = 0.2e-3 m, rho = 1000, g = 9.8, cos0 = 1; T = (0.05 x 0.2e-3 x 1000 x 9.8)/(2 x 1) = (0.098)/2 = 0.049 N/m; Final answer: 0.049 N/m." },
        { q: "State Bernoulli's principle and write Bernoulli's equation, explaining each term.", a: "Bernoulli's principle: for an ideal (non-viscous, incompressible) fluid in streamline flow, the sum of pressure energy, kinetic energy and potential energy per unit volume remains constant; Equation: P + (1/2)rho*v^2 + rho*g*h = constant; P is pressure energy per unit volume, (1/2)rho*v^2 is kinetic energy per unit volume, rho*g*h is potential energy per unit volume; It expresses conservation of energy for flowing fluid." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Kinetic Theory of Gases and Radiation",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter explains gas behaviour from molecular motion and treats thermal radiation laws. The central result is that pressure relates to mean square molecular speed, and temperature measures average kinetic energy. Radiation topics cover blackbody emission, Stefan-Boltzmann, Wien's and Kirchhoff's laws.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Pressure of gas and RMS speed", e: "Gas pressure arises from molecular collisions with container walls and is related to the mean square molecular speed through P = (1/3)(N/V)m*vbar^2." },
        { t: "Degrees of freedom and law of equipartition", e: "The number of independent ways a molecule can store energy (degrees of freedom) determines its energy via the equipartition law, which assigns (1/2)kT per degree of freedom." },
        { t: "Mean free path", e: "The mean free path is the average distance a gas molecule travels between successive collisions with other molecules." },
        { t: "Blackbody radiation and emissive power", e: "A blackbody absorbs and emits all wavelengths of radiation ideally, and its emissive power is the energy radiated per unit area per unit time." },
        { t: "Stefan-Boltzmann law", e: "This law states that the energy radiated per unit area per unit time by a blackbody is proportional to the fourth power of its absolute temperature, E = sigma*T^4." },
        { t: "Wien's displacement and Kirchhoff's law", e: "Wien's displacement law gives the wavelength of peak emission as inversely proportional to temperature, while Kirchhoff's law states a good absorber of radiation is also a good emitter at the same temperature." }
      ],
      core: "Derivation of P = (1/3)(N/V)m*vbar^2, equipartition of energy, Stefan-Boltzmann numericals.",
      prep: "Memorize vrms = sqrt(3RT/M) and the radiation laws with their constants; practice deriving gas pressure and applying Stefan's law E = sigma*T^4.",
      questions: [
        { q: "Calculate the RMS speed of oxygen molecules at 27 degree C. (M = 32e-3 kg/mol, R = 8.314 J/mol K).", a: "T = 27 + 273 = 300 K; vrms = sqrt(3RT/M) = sqrt(3 x 8.314 x 300 / 32e-3); Numerator = 3 x 8.314 x 300 = 7482.6; Divide by 0.032 = 233831; sqrt = 483.6 m/s; Final answer: approximately 484 m/s." },
        { q: "State Stefan-Boltzmann law. A blackbody at 500 K radiates energy at rate E1. Find the ratio of rates when temperature is raised to 1000 K.", a: "Stefan-Boltzmann law: energy radiated per unit area per unit time by a blackbody is proportional to the fourth power of its absolute temperature, E = sigma*T^4; Ratio E2/E1 = (T2/T1)^4 = (1000/500)^4 = 2^4 = 16; Final answer: the rate increases 16 times." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Thermodynamics",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter deals with heat, work and internal energy through the laws of thermodynamics. The first law is energy conservation (dQ = dU + dW), and the second law sets the direction of processes and limits of heat engines. Various processes (isothermal, adiabatic) and the Carnot cycle define maximum possible efficiency.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Zeroth and first law of thermodynamics", e: "The zeroth law defines thermal equilibrium and temperature, while the first law states heat supplied equals the increase in internal energy plus work done, dQ = dU + dW." },
        { t: "Isothermal and adiabatic processes", e: "An isothermal process occurs at constant temperature so internal energy is unchanged, while an adiabatic process has no heat exchange so all energy change appears as work." },
        { t: "Thermodynamic work and PV diagrams", e: "Work done by a gas equals the area under the curve on a pressure-volume (PV) diagram, calculated as the integral of P dV." },
        { t: "Heat engines and refrigerators", e: "A heat engine converts part of the heat absorbed from a hot reservoir into work while rejecting the rest to a cold reservoir, and a refrigerator does the reverse using external work input." },
        { t: "Second law and Carnot cycle", e: "The second law limits how much heat can be converted to work, and the Carnot cycle is the idealized reversible cycle that gives the maximum possible efficiency between two temperatures." },
        { t: "Efficiency and coefficient of performance", e: "Efficiency eta = 1 - T2/T1 measures a heat engine's work output per heat input, while coefficient of performance measures a refrigerator's heat removed per work input." }
      ],
      core: "First law applications, work done in isothermal/adiabatic processes, Carnot efficiency numericals.",
      prep: "Know dQ = dU + dW sign conventions and the Carnot efficiency eta = 1 - T2/T1; practice work-done integrals and engine efficiency problems.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A Carnot engine operates between 500 K and 300 K. Calculate its efficiency and the work done if it absorbs 1000 J of heat.", a: "Efficiency eta = 1 - T2/T1 = 1 - 300/500 = 1 - 0.6 = 0.4 or 40%; Work done W = eta x Q1 = 0.4 x 1000 = 400 J; Final answer: efficiency 40%, work 400 J." },
        { q: "State the first law of thermodynamics and apply it to an isothermal process.", a: "First law: heat supplied to a system equals the increase in internal energy plus work done by the system, dQ = dU + dW; In an isothermal process temperature is constant so for an ideal gas dU = 0; Therefore dQ = dW, meaning all heat supplied is converted into work done by the gas; Work done W = nRT*ln(V2/V1)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Oscillations",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter covers simple harmonic motion where restoring force is proportional to displacement. The key idea is the SHM equation and its energy exchange between kinetic and potential forms. Systems like springs and simple pendulums, plus damped oscillations, are analyzed.",
      topics: [
        { t: "SHM and its differential equation", e: "Simple harmonic motion satisfies d^2x/dt^2 = -w^2*x, meaning the restoring acceleration is always proportional and opposite to displacement." },
        { t: "Displacement, velocity and acceleration in SHM", e: "In SHM, x = A*sin(wt), velocity v = A*w*cos(wt) = w*sqrt(A^2 - x^2), and acceleration a = -w^2*x, all oscillating in a fixed phase relationship." },
        { t: "Energy in SHM", e: "Total mechanical energy in SHM, (1/2)m*w^2*A^2, stays constant while continuously converting between kinetic energy and potential energy as the particle oscillates." },
        { t: "Simple pendulum and spring systems", e: "A simple pendulum has period T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g) and a mass-spring system has period T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k), both examples of SHM with different restoring forces." },
        { t: "Phase and epoch", e: "Phase describes the state of oscillation (position and direction of motion) at a given time, and epoch is the initial phase angle at t = 0." },
        { t: "Damped and forced oscillations, resonance", e: "Damped oscillations lose amplitude over time due to resistive forces, forced oscillations are driven by an external periodic force, and resonance occurs when the driving frequency matches the natural frequency, producing maximum amplitude." }
      ],
      core: "Derivation of SHM differential equation and energy expressions; period of pendulum and spring numericals.",
      prep: "Memorize T = 2*pi*sqrt(m/k) and T = 2*pi*sqrt(L/g); practice energy at a given displacement and velocity v = w*sqrt(A^2 - x^2).",
      questions: [
        { q: "A particle performs SHM of amplitude 0.05 m and period 2 s. Find its maximum velocity and maximum acceleration.", a: "Angular frequency w = 2*pi/T = 2*pi/2 = pi rad/s = 3.14 rad/s; Max velocity vmax = A*w = 0.05 x 3.14 = 0.157 m/s; Max acceleration amax = A*w^2 = 0.05 x (3.14)^2 = 0.05 x 9.87 = 0.493 m/s^2; Final answer: vmax = 0.157 m/s, amax = 0.493 m/s^2." },
        { q: "Derive the expression for kinetic and potential energy of a particle performing SHM.", a: "Displacement x = A*sin(wt), velocity v = A*w*cos(wt); KE = (1/2)mv^2 = (1/2)m*A^2*w^2*cos^2(wt) = (1/2)m*w^2(A^2 - x^2); PE = (1/2)kx^2 = (1/2)m*w^2*x^2 (since k = m*w^2); Total energy = KE + PE = (1/2)m*w^2*A^2 which is constant; This shows energy continually converts between kinetic and potential forms." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Superposition of Waves",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter studies what happens when two or more waves overlap, producing interference, stationary waves and beats. The key idea is that stationary waves form from two identical waves travelling opposite ways, creating fixed nodes and antinodes. Vibrations of strings and air columns give the harmonics of musical instruments.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Principle of superposition", e: "When two or more waves overlap at a point, the resultant displacement is the algebraic sum of the individual wave displacements at that point." },
        { t: "Stationary waves and nodes/antinodes", e: "Stationary (standing) waves form when two identical waves travel in opposite directions and interfere, creating fixed points of zero displacement (nodes) and maximum displacement (antinodes)." },
        { t: "Vibrations of strings and harmonics", e: "A stretched string vibrates at a fundamental frequency n = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/m) and at integer multiples called harmonics, determined by the number of loops formed." },
        { t: "Vibrations in air columns (open/closed pipes)", e: "Open pipes support all harmonics of the fundamental frequency, while closed pipes (closed at one end) support only odd harmonics, due to the boundary conditions at the pipe ends." },
        { t: "Beats and beat frequency", e: "Beats are periodic rises and falls in loudness produced when two waves of slightly different frequencies superpose, with beat frequency equal to the difference |n1 - n2|." },
        { t: "End correction and resonance", e: "End correction accounts for the extra effective length beyond a pipe's open end where antinode formation is slightly displaced, and resonance in air columns is used to measure the speed of sound experimentally." }
      ],
      core: "Formation of stationary waves, harmonics of strings and pipes, beats numericals.",
      prep: "Learn fundamental frequency formulas for string (n = (1/2L)sqrt(T/m)), open and closed pipes; practice beat frequency and harmonic ratio problems.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A string of length 0.5 m and linear density 0.01 kg/m is under tension 100 N. Find its fundamental frequency.", a: "Fundamental frequency n = (1/2L)*sqrt(T/m); Substitute L = 0.5, T = 100, m = 0.01; sqrt(T/m) = sqrt(100/0.01) = sqrt(10000) = 100; n = (1/(2x0.5)) x 100 = (1/1) x 100 = 100 Hz; Final answer: 100 Hz." },
        { q: "Explain the formation of beats and derive the expression for beat frequency.", a: "When two waves of slightly different frequencies n1 and n2 superpose, the resultant amplitude rises and falls periodically producing beats; Resultant of y1 = a*sin(2*pi*n1*t) and y2 = a*sin(2*pi*n2*t) is y = 2a*cos(2*pi*((n1-n2)/2)t)*sin(2*pi*((n1+n2)/2)t); Amplitude term 2a*cos(pi(n1-n2)t) varies with frequency |n1 - n2|; One beat occurs per cycle of amplitude so beat frequency = |n1 - n2|." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Wave Optics",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter treats light as a wave to explain interference, diffraction and polarization. Huygens' principle and Young's double slit experiment demonstrate the wave nature and measure wavelength. Diffraction and polarization reveal further wave behaviour and applications like Brewster's law.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Huygens' principle and wavefronts", e: "Huygens' principle states every point on a wavefront acts as a source of secondary wavelets, and the new wavefront is the envelope of these wavelets, explaining propagation, reflection and refraction." },
        { t: "Young's double slit experiment and fringe width", e: "Young's double slit experiment produces interference fringes of width W = lambda*D/d, demonstrating the wave nature of light and allowing wavelength measurement." },
        { t: "Conditions for constructive/destructive interference", e: "Constructive interference (bright fringes) occurs when the path difference between two waves is an integer multiple of wavelength, and destructive interference (dark fringes) occurs for odd multiples of half-wavelength." },
        { t: "Diffraction at a single slit", e: "Diffraction is the bending of light around obstacles or through narrow slits, producing a central bright band flanked by alternating dark and bright fringes of decreasing intensity." },
        { t: "Polarization and Brewster's law", e: "Polarization restricts light vibrations to a single plane, and Brewster's law gives the angle of incidence, tan(iB) = n, at which reflected light becomes completely plane polarized." },
        { t: "Resolving power", e: "Resolving power is the ability of an optical instrument to distinguish between two closely spaced objects, limited fundamentally by diffraction." }
      ],
      core: "YDSE fringe width derivation and numericals; conditions for bright/dark fringes; Brewster's law.",
      prep: "Memorize fringe width W = lambda*D/d and path difference conditions; practice YDSE numericals and Brewster's angle calculation.",
      questions: [
        { q: "In Young's double slit experiment slits are 1 mm apart and screen is 1 m away. Light of wavelength 600 nm is used. Find the fringe width.", a: "Fringe width W = lambda*D/d; Substitute lambda = 600e-9 m, D = 1 m, d = 1e-3 m; W = (600e-9 x 1)/(1e-3) = 600e-6 = 6e-4 m = 0.6 mm; Final answer: 0.6 mm." },
        { q: "State Brewster's law and calculate the Brewster angle for glass of refractive index 1.5.", a: "Brewster's law: when unpolarized light is incident at the polarizing angle, the reflected light is completely plane polarized and the tangent of the polarizing angle equals the refractive index, tan(iB) = n; For n = 1.5, iB = tan inverse(1.5) = 56.3 degrees; Final answer: Brewster angle = 56.3 degrees." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Electrostatics",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter deals with charges at rest, the electric field and potential they create, and capacitance. Gauss's law provides a powerful way to find fields of symmetric charge distributions. Capacitors store energy and combine in series and parallel.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Coulomb's law and electric field", e: "Coulomb's law gives the force between two point charges as F = kq1q2/r^2, and the electric field is the force per unit charge that this creates in the surrounding space." },
        { t: "Gauss's law and applications", e: "Gauss's law relates the total electric flux through a closed surface to the enclosed charge, flux = q/epsilon0, and simplifies finding fields of symmetric charge distributions like spheres, wires and sheets." },
        { t: "Electric potential and potential energy", e: "Electric potential is the work done per unit charge to bring a test charge from infinity to a point, and potential energy is the work done to assemble a system of charges." },
        { t: "Capacitors and capacitance", e: "A capacitor stores charge on two conductors separated by a dielectric, with capacitance C = Q/V defining how much charge it stores per unit potential difference." },
        { t: "Combination of capacitors", e: "Capacitors in series combine as 1/C = 1/C1 + 1/C2 + ... (reducing total capacitance) while capacitors in parallel combine as C = C1 + C2 + ... (adding capacitance)." },
        { t: "Energy stored in a capacitor and dielectrics", e: "The energy stored in a charged capacitor is U = (1/2)CV^2, and inserting a dielectric between the plates increases capacitance by reducing the effective field." }
      ],
      core: "Gauss's law applications, capacitor combinations, energy stored = (1/2)CV^2 numericals.",
      prep: "Learn Gauss's law field expressions for sphere/wire/sheet and capacitor combination formulas; practice energy and equivalent capacitance problems.",
      questions: [
        { q: "Two capacitors of 4 uF and 6 uF are connected in series across a 100 V supply. Find the equivalent capacitance and energy stored.", a: "Series: 1/C = 1/4 + 1/6 = 3/12 + 2/12 = 5/12, so C = 12/5 = 2.4 uF; Energy U = (1/2)CV^2 = (1/2) x 2.4e-6 x (100)^2 = (1/2) x 2.4e-6 x 10000 = 0.012 J; Final answer: C = 2.4 uF, U = 0.012 J (12 mJ)." },
        { q: "State Gauss's law and use it to derive the electric field due to an infinitely long charged wire.", a: "Gauss's law: the total electric flux through a closed surface equals the enclosed charge divided by epsilon0, flux = q/epsilon0; Consider a cylindrical Gaussian surface of radius r and length L around a wire of linear charge density lambda; Flux through curved surface = E x 2*pi*r*L and enclosed charge = lambda*L; So E x 2*pi*r*L = lambda*L/epsilon0; Therefore E = lambda/(2*pi*epsilon0*r), directed radially." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Current Electricity",
      wt: "~4 marks",
      explain: "This chapter analyzes circuits using Kirchhoff's laws and bridge arrangements. The key tools are Kirchhoff's current and voltage laws for complex networks and the Wheatstone bridge balance condition. Potentiometer and meter bridge are precision measurement applications.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Kirchhoff's laws", e: "Kirchhoff's current law states the sum of currents entering a junction equals the sum leaving it, and Kirchhoff's voltage law states the sum of potential differences around any closed loop is zero, enabling analysis of complex circuits." },
        { t: "Wheatstone bridge and balance condition", e: "A Wheatstone bridge compares an unknown resistance against known ones, and is balanced (zero galvanometer deflection) when P/Q = R/S." },
        { t: "Meter bridge", e: "A meter bridge is a practical form of the Wheatstone bridge using a uniform wire of length 1 m to measure an unknown resistance from the balancing point." },
        { t: "Potentiometer and its applications", e: "A potentiometer uses the balancing length along a uniform current-carrying wire to measure EMF or potential difference accurately without drawing current from the source." },
        { t: "Comparison of EMFs", e: "Two cells' EMFs can be compared using a potentiometer by finding their respective balancing lengths, since E1/E2 = L1/L2." },
        { t: "Internal resistance measurement", e: "The internal resistance of a cell can be found using a potentiometer by comparing balancing lengths with and without a known resistance connected across the cell." }
      ],
      core: "Wheatstone bridge balance derivation, potentiometer EMF comparison, Kirchhoff's law numericals.",
      prep: "Master the balance condition P/Q = R/S and potentiometer EMF ratio E1/E2 = L1/L2; practice circuit problems applying Kirchhoff's laws.",
      questions: [
        { q: "In a Wheatstone bridge P = 10 ohm, Q = 20 ohm, R = 15 ohm. Find S for the bridge to be balanced.", a: "Balance condition: P/Q = R/S; So S = R x Q/P = 15 x 20/10 = 15 x 2 = 30 ohm; Final answer: S = 30 ohm." },
        { q: "Describe how a potentiometer is used to compare the EMFs of two cells.", a: "The two cells are connected one at a time to the galvanometer through a two-way key, with a steady current in the potentiometer wire; For cell 1 the balancing length L1 is found where galvanometer shows no deflection, so E1 = k*L1; For cell 2 the balancing length L2 gives E2 = k*L2; Dividing, E1/E2 = L1/L2; Thus EMFs are compared from the ratio of balancing lengths without drawing current from the cells." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Magnetic Fields due to Electric Current",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter explains the magnetic field produced by currents using the Biot-Savart law and Ampere's law. These give fields of wires, loops and solenoids. The chapter also covers forces on currents and the working of moving coil galvanometers and their conversion to ammeters and voltmeters.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Biot-Savart law", e: "The Biot-Savart law gives the magnetic field dB produced at a point by a small current element Idl as dB = (mu0/4*pi)*(I*dl x r)/r^3, where r is the vector from the element to the point. The magnitude depends on the current, the length of the element, the sine of the angle between dl and r, and inversely on the square of the distance. It is the magnetic analogue of Coulomb's law, letting us build up the total field of any current distribution by integration (superposition). Direction of dB is found using the right-hand rule, perpendicular to the plane containing dl and r. This law is the fundamental starting point for deriving the fields of wires, loops and solenoids. It applies strictly to steady currents and is verified by comparison with Ampere's law results for symmetric cases." },
        { t: "Magnetic field of a circular loop and straight wire", e: "Using Biot-Savart's law, the magnetic field at the centre of a circular loop of radius r carrying current I is B = mu0*I/(2r), directed along the axis as given by the right-hand rule. On the axis at distance x from the centre, the field is B = mu0*I*r^2/[2(r^2+x^2)^(3/2)], reducing to the centre value when x = 0. For an infinitely long straight wire, the field at perpendicular distance r is B = mu0*I/(2*pi*r), forming concentric circles around the wire. These results show field strength increases with current and decreases with distance from the source. Such formulas are used to design coils, calculate forces between parallel wires, and in instruments like tangent galvanometers." },
        { t: "Ampere's circuital law and solenoid", e: "Ampere's circuital law states that the line integral of the magnetic field around any closed loop equals mu0 times the net current enclosed by that loop, written as the integral of B.dl = mu0*I_enclosed. It is the magnetic analogue of Gauss's law and greatly simplifies finding fields in situations with high symmetry. Applying it to a long solenoid with n turns per unit length carrying current I gives a uniform field inside, B = mu0*n*I, directed along the axis, and essentially zero field outside. For a toroid, a similar argument gives B = mu0*N*I/(2*pi*r) inside the core. This law underlies the design of electromagnets, solenoids used as switches (relays), and MRI machines where uniform strong fields are required." },
        { t: "Force on a current carrying conductor", e: "A current-carrying conductor placed in a magnetic field experiences a force F = I*L x B, where L is the length vector along the current direction and B is the external field; the magnitude is F = BIL*sin(theta). This force arises because the field exerts a force on each moving charge (F = qv x B) constituting the current, summed over the conductor. The direction is given by Fleming's left-hand rule or the right-hand slap rule. Two parallel current-carrying wires exert forces on each other, attractive if currents are in the same direction and repulsive if opposite, which is used to define the SI ampere. This principle is the basis of electric motors, loudspeakers and moving-coil meters, where current-carrying coils in magnetic fields produce mechanical motion or torque." },
        { t: "Moving coil galvanometer", e: "A moving coil (galvanometer) instrument consists of a rectangular coil of wire suspended in a radial magnetic field between the poles of a permanent magnet, with a restoring spring providing a controlling torque. When current I flows through the coil of N turns and area A, it experiences a deflecting torque tau = N*I*A*B, which is balanced by the spring's restoring torque k*theta, giving deflection theta proportional to current, theta = (NAB/k)*I. This linear relationship between deflection and current allows the scale to be uniformly calibrated. The sensitivity of the galvanometer depends on N, A, B and the spring constant k, and can be increased by using more turns or a stronger field. Galvanometers are the basic sensing element converted into ammeters and voltmeters by adding appropriate shunt or series resistances." },
        { t: "Conversion of galvanometer to ammeter/voltmeter", e: "A galvanometer of resistance G that gives full-scale deflection for current Ig can be converted into an ammeter of range I by connecting a low-value shunt resistance S in parallel, where S = Ig*G/(I - Ig); the shunt diverts most of the current, protecting the galvanometer coil. To convert the same galvanometer into a voltmeter of range V, a high-value resistance R is connected in series, where R = (V/Ig) - G, limiting the current through the coil to Ig at full-scale voltage V. An ideal ammeter should have very low resistance so it does not affect the circuit current, hence the shunt is small; an ideal voltmeter should have very high resistance so it draws negligible current, hence the series resistance is large. These conversions make a single sensitive galvanometer usable for a wide range of measurement tasks." }
      ],
      core: "Biot-Savart and Ampere's law field derivations; galvanometer conversion numericals.",
      prep: "Learn field formulas B = mu0*I/(2*pi*r), B = mu0*nI for solenoid, and shunt/series resistance formulas; practice ammeter and voltmeter conversions.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A galvanometer of resistance 50 ohm shows full scale deflection for 2 mA. How can it be converted into an ammeter of range 1 A?", a: "A shunt resistance S is connected in parallel; S = Ig*G/(I - Ig); Ig = 2e-3 A, G = 50 ohm, I = 1 A; S = (2e-3 x 50)/(1 - 2e-3) = 0.1/0.998 = 0.1002 ohm; Final answer: connect a shunt of about 0.1 ohm in parallel." },
        { q: "State Ampere's circuital law and use it to find the magnetic field inside a long solenoid.", a: "Ampere's law: the line integral of magnetic field around a closed loop equals mu0 times the net current enclosed, integral of B.dl = mu0*I; For a solenoid of n turns per unit length carrying current I, choose a rectangular loop of length L partly inside; Only the inside path contributes, so B*L = mu0*(n*L)*I; Therefore B = mu0*n*I, uniform inside and directed along the axis." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Magnetic Materials",
      wt: "~3 marks",
      explain: "This chapter classifies materials by their magnetic behaviour and explains magnetism at the atomic level. The key ideas are magnetic moment of an orbiting electron and the distinctions between dia-, para- and ferromagnetic materials. Magnetization, susceptibility and hysteresis are quantified.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Magnetic moment of orbital electron", e: "An electron revolving around the nucleus in a circular orbit constitutes a tiny current loop, I = e*f, where f is the revolution frequency, and this loop has an associated orbital magnetic moment mu = I*A = (e*v*r)/2 for orbit radius r and speed v. This magnetic moment is directed perpendicular to the plane of the orbit, opposite to the orbital angular momentum direction because the electron is negatively charged. Quantum mechanically, the orbital angular momentum is quantized as L = n*h/(2*pi), leading to quantized values of magnetic moment including the Bohr magneton, mu_B = eh/(4*pi*m), the fundamental unit of atomic magnetism. This microscopic magnetic moment is the origin of the macroscopic magnetic properties of materials. Such moments, along with electron spin, combine to determine whether an atom has a net magnetic moment." },
        { t: "Magnetization and magnetic intensity", e: "Magnetization M is defined as the net magnetic moment per unit volume of a material, M = (net magnetic moment)/volume, and it quantifies how strongly a material responds to an external magnetic field. Magnetic intensity (magnetizing field) H is the external field applied by a current-carrying coil, independent of the medium, with the relation B = mu0(H + M) for the total field inside the material. H has the same units as M (A/m) and is analogous to the electric field E produced by free charges, while B includes the material's response. Magnetization arises from alignment of atomic magnetic dipoles under the applied field, and its magnitude and direction differ for diamagnetic, paramagnetic and ferromagnetic substances. This concept is essential for understanding why different materials produce very different total fields for the same applied H." },
        { t: "Magnetic susceptibility and permeability", e: "Magnetic susceptibility chi is defined as the ratio of magnetization to the applied magnetizing field, chi = M/H, and indicates how easily a material gets magnetized. Relative permeability mu_r relates to susceptibility by mu_r = 1 + chi, and absolute permeability is mu = mu0*mu_r, giving B = mu*H inside the material. Diamagnetic materials have small negative chi (mu_r slightly less than 1), paramagnetic materials have small positive chi (mu_r slightly more than 1), and ferromagnetic materials have large positive chi (mu_r much greater than 1). These quantities let engineers predict how much a given material will concentrate or weaken magnetic flux for applications like transformer cores. Measuring chi experimentally also helps classify unknown materials and assess their suitability for magnetic devices." },
        { t: "Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism", e: "Diamagnetic materials (e.g., copper, bismuth, water) have no permanent atomic dipoles; an applied field induces a weak moment opposing the field, so they are weakly repelled and have small negative susceptibility that is temperature-independent. Paramagnetic materials (e.g., aluminium, platinum, oxygen) have atoms with permanent magnetic dipoles that are randomly oriented by thermal motion; an applied field partially aligns them, giving weak attraction and small positive susceptibility that decreases with temperature. Ferromagnetic materials (e.g., iron, nickel, cobalt) have strong interatomic interactions that align dipoles into domains, producing large magnetization, strong attraction, large susceptibility, and retention of magnetism after the field is removed (hysteresis). The underlying cause in all three cases is the behaviour of electron orbital and spin magnetic moments under thermal agitation and interatomic exchange interactions. This classification explains why some materials make good permanent magnets, some make good electromagnet cores, and others are magnetically inert." },
        { t: "Curie's law", e: "Curie's law states that for a paramagnetic material, the magnetic susceptibility is inversely proportional to the absolute temperature, chi = C/T, where C is the Curie constant characteristic of the material. This arises because higher temperature increases thermal agitation that randomizes the atomic dipoles, reducing the net alignment (and hence magnetization) produced by a given applied field. As temperature decreases, susceptibility increases because dipoles align more easily with less thermal disruption. Ferromagnetic materials obey a related Curie-Weiss law above a critical temperature called the Curie temperature, above which they lose their ferromagnetic ordering and behave paramagnetically. Curie's law is experimentally verified by measuring susceptibility of paramagnetic salts at different temperatures and is important in understanding temperature-dependent magnetic behaviour in materials science." },
        { t: "Hysteresis loop", e: "A hysteresis loop is the closed curve obtained by plotting magnetic flux density B against magnetizing field H as a ferromagnetic material is taken through a complete cycle of magnetization, demagnetization, reverse magnetization and reverse demagnetization. The loop shows that B lags behind H, meaning the material retains some magnetization (retentivity) even when H is reduced to zero, and requires a reverse field (coercivity) to bring B back to zero. The area enclosed by the hysteresis loop represents the energy dissipated as heat per unit volume per cycle due to internal friction between domains. Materials with large hysteresis loop area (like steel) are used for permanent magnets, while materials with thin, narrow loops (like soft iron) are preferred for transformer cores and electromagnets to minimize energy loss. Understanding hysteresis is crucial for selecting appropriate magnetic materials for different electrical and electronic applications." }
      ],
      core: "Classification of magnetic materials with properties; susceptibility/permeability relations; Curie's law.",
      prep: "Tabulate the three material types with susceptibility signs and examples; learn mu_r = 1 + chi and Curie's law chi proportional to 1/T.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A material has magnetic susceptibility 499. Find its relative permeability.", a: "Relation: mu_r = 1 + chi; Substitute chi = 499; mu_r = 1 + 499 = 500; Final answer: relative permeability = 500." },
        { q: "Distinguish between diamagnetic, paramagnetic and ferromagnetic materials.", a: "Diamagnetic: weakly repelled by magnets, susceptibility small and negative, no permanent dipoles, e.g. copper, bismuth; Paramagnetic: weakly attracted, susceptibility small and positive, have weak permanent dipoles aligning with field, e.g. aluminium, platinum; Ferromagnetic: strongly attracted, susceptibility large and positive, have domains giving strong magnetization and hysteresis, e.g. iron, nickel, cobalt." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Electromagnetic Induction",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter describes how changing magnetic flux induces EMF, governed by Faraday's and Lenz's laws. Self and mutual inductance quantify induced EMF from changing currents. The chapter underlies generators, transformers and eddy current applications.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Faraday's laws of induction", e: "Faraday's first law states that whenever the magnetic flux linked with a circuit changes, an EMF is induced in it, and this induced EMF lasts only as long as the change in flux continues. Faraday's second law quantifies this, stating the induced EMF is directly proportional to the rate of change of magnetic flux linkage, e = -N*d(phi)/dt, where N is the number of turns and phi is the flux through one turn. The negative sign (explained by Lenz's law) indicates the induced EMF opposes the change producing it. These laws were established experimentally by moving magnets near coils and observing induced currents, forming the foundation of electromagnetic induction. They explain the working of generators, transformers, and induction-based sensors, converting mechanical motion or changing currents into usable electrical energy." },
        { t: "Lenz's law and energy conservation", e: "Lenz's law states that the direction of an induced current is always such that it opposes the change in magnetic flux that produces it, which is mathematically expressed through the negative sign in Faraday's equation, e = -N*d(phi)/dt. This opposition means work must be done against the induced effects to change the flux, and this work is the source of the electrical energy that appears in the circuit, ensuring the law of conservation of energy is not violated. For example, pushing a magnet into a coil induces a current whose magnetic field repels the magnet, requiring extra force and hence extra work from the person pushing it. If induced currents instead aided the change, flux would grow without added energy input, creating a perpetual energy source, which is physically impossible. Lenz's law thus provides both the direction of induced current and a deep physical justification rooted in energy conservation, and is essential for predicting current directions in all EMI problems." },
        { t: "Motional EMF", e: "Motional EMF is the EMF induced in a conductor when it moves through a magnetic field such that the area of the circuit (and hence flux) changes with time. For a straight rod of length L moving with velocity v perpendicular to a uniform field B, the motional EMF is e = B*L*v, derived either from the rate of change of flux or from the magnetic force (F = qv x B) pushing free charges in the rod to one end, creating a potential difference. This separation of charge continues until the induced electric field balances the magnetic force on charges, establishing a steady EMF as long as the motion continues. Motional EMF explains the working of simple generators where a rod or coil moves (or rotates) in a magnetic field to produce continuous electrical energy. It is a direct consequence of Faraday's law applied to a changing-area circuit rather than a changing field strength." },
        { t: "Self inductance and mutual inductance", e: "Self inductance L of a coil is the property by which a changing current in the coil induces an EMF in itself, defined by e = -L*dI/dt, or equivalently by flux linkage N*phi = L*I; for a long solenoid, L = mu0*n^2*A*l, showing it depends on geometry and the medium. Mutual inductance M between two coils is the property by which a changing current in one coil induces an EMF in a neighbouring coil, given by e2 = -M*dI1/dt, with M depending on the coils' turns, geometry, separation and relative orientation. Both quantities arise from Faraday's and Lenz's laws applied to circuits that are either self-linked or magnetically coupled to another circuit. Self inductance causes opposition to any change of current in a circuit (analogous to inertia in mechanics), while mutual inductance is the basis of energy transfer between coupled circuits. These concepts underlie the operation of inductors, transformers, and induction coils used throughout electrical engineering." },
        { t: "Energy stored in an inductor", e: "When current in an inductor increases from zero to I, work must be done against the back-EMF (e = -L*dI/dt) generated by the inductor, and this work is stored as magnetic potential energy in the inductor's field. The energy stored is given by U = (1/2)*L*I^2, analogous to the formula for energy in a capacitor, (1/2)CV^2, and in a moving mass, (1/2)mv^2. This energy resides in the magnetic field within and around the inductor and can be recovered when the current (and hence field) decreases, for example producing sparks when a circuit with an inductor is suddenly opened. This stored energy explains phenomena like the sudden voltage spike across a switch when an inductive circuit (like a relay coil) is switched off. Understanding this energy is essential for designing circuits with inductors, including power supplies, filters and energy storage systems." },
        { t: "Eddy currents and applications", e: "Eddy currents are closed loops of induced current that circulate within the body of a bulk conductor when it experiences a changing magnetic flux, arising from the same Faraday-Lenz principle as currents in wires but distributed through the solid material. By Lenz's law, these currents oppose the change in flux causing them, dissipating energy as heat due to the resistance of the material, which can be a wasteful loss (e.g., in transformer cores) or a useful effect. To minimize unwanted eddy current losses in transformers and motors, cores are laminated with thin insulated sheets, which increases resistance to the circulating currents and reduces their magnitude. Useful applications of eddy currents include electromagnetic braking in trains, induction furnaces for melting metals, speedometers, and metal detectors. The strength of eddy currents depends on the rate of flux change, the conductivity of the material, and the geometry (size) of the conducting body." }
      ],
      core: "Faraday-Lenz law derivations, self/mutual inductance and induced EMF numericals, energy = (1/2)LI^2.",
      prep: "Learn e = -d(phi)/dt, e = -L dI/dt, and energy stored formula; practice flux-change and inductance numericals.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A coil of 500 turns experiences a flux change from 2e-3 Wb to 6e-3 Wb in 0.1 s. Find the induced EMF.", a: "Induced EMF e = -N*d(phi)/dt; Change in flux = 6e-3 - 2e-3 = 4e-3 Wb; e = -500 x (4e-3/0.1) = -500 x 0.04 = -20 V; Magnitude of EMF = 20 V; Final answer: 20 V." },
        { q: "State Faraday's laws of electromagnetic induction and explain Lenz's law.", a: "Faraday's first law: an EMF is induced in a circuit whenever the magnetic flux linked with it changes; Faraday's second law: the magnitude of induced EMF equals the rate of change of magnetic flux, e = N*d(phi)/dt; Lenz's law: the direction of induced current is such that it opposes the change in flux that causes it; This opposition ensures conservation of energy, giving the negative sign e = -N*d(phi)/dt." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "AC Circuits",
      wt: "~4 marks",
      explain: "This chapter analyzes circuits driven by alternating voltage containing resistors, inductors and capacitors. The key ideas are reactance, impedance and phase difference between current and voltage. Series LCR resonance and power factor are important outcomes.",
      topics: [
        { t: "AC through resistor, inductor and capacitor", e: "When an alternating voltage v = v0*sin(wt) is applied across a pure resistor, the current is i = i0*sin(wt), in phase with the voltage, since Ohm's law v = iR holds instantaneously. Across a pure inductor, the current lags the voltage by 90 degrees, i = i0*sin(wt - pi/2), because the back-EMF (-L*di/dt) opposes rapid changes in current. Across a pure capacitor, the current leads the voltage by 90 degrees, i = i0*sin(wt + pi/2), since the capacitor must first accumulate charge before building up voltage. These differing phase relationships arise purely from the nature of each element's response to a time-varying current, and they determine how voltage and current waveforms combine in circuits containing more than one type of element. Understanding these individual behaviours is the foundation for analyzing series LCR circuits." },
        { t: "Reactance and impedance", e: "Reactance is the effective opposition offered by a pure inductor or capacitor to the flow of alternating current, without dissipating energy (unlike resistance). Inductive reactance is XL = w*L = 2*pi*f*L, increasing with frequency, while capacitive reactance is XC = 1/(w*C) = 1/(2*pi*f*C), decreasing with frequency. Impedance Z is the total effective opposition of a circuit containing resistance and reactance combined, given for a series LCR circuit by Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL - XC)^2), with current amplitude i0 = v0/Z. Unlike pure resistance, impedance involves a phase angle phi = tan inverse((XL-XC)/R) between voltage and current, since reactive elements store and release energy instead of dissipating it. These quantities are essential in AC circuit design for filters, tuned circuits and power transmission systems." },
        { t: "Series LCR circuit and phasors", e: "A series LCR circuit contains a resistor, inductor and capacitor connected in series with an AC source, carrying the same current but with voltages across each element differing in phase. Phasor diagrams represent these voltages as rotating vectors: VR along the current direction, VL leading the current by 90 degrees, and VC lagging the current by 90 degrees; the resultant voltage is the vector sum, V = sqrt(VR^2 + (VL-VC)^2). This phasor approach gives the total impedance Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL-XC)^2) and the phase angle phi between voltage and current, tan(phi) = (XL-XC)/R. When XL > XC the circuit is inductive (voltage leads current), and when XC > XL the circuit is capacitive (current leads voltage). This analysis is essential for predicting current amplitude, phase, and power in AC networks used in radios, filters, and power systems." },
        { t: "Resonance in LCR circuit", e: "Resonance occurs in a series LCR circuit when the inductive reactance exactly equals the capacitive reactance, XL = XC, making the impedance purely resistive, Z = R, its minimum possible value, so current is maximum, I = V/R. The resonant angular frequency is w0 = 1/sqrt(LC), giving resonant frequency f0 = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(LC)), at which the circuit's natural oscillation frequency matches the driving frequency. At resonance, voltage and current are in phase (phi = 0), and the circuit draws maximum power from the source for a given applied voltage. This phenomenon is exploited in radio and television tuning circuits to selectively amplify signals of a desired frequency while rejecting others, and in filter circuits for signal processing." },
        { t: "Power in AC circuit and power factor", e: "The instantaneous power in an AC circuit is p = v*i, but the average power over a full cycle for a circuit with phase angle phi between voltage and current is Pavg = Vrms*Irms*cos(phi), where cos(phi) is called the power factor. For a purely resistive circuit, phi = 0 and power factor is 1, giving maximum power transfer; for a purely reactive circuit (pure L or C), phi = 90 degrees and power factor is zero, meaning no net power is consumed since energy is alternately stored and returned each cycle. Power factor indicates how effectively electrical power is being converted into useful work, and a low power factor means more current is needed to deliver the same real power, increasing transmission losses. Industries use capacitor banks to improve (increase) the power factor of inductive loads like motors, reducing wasted reactive power and lowering electricity costs. Correct power factor management is essential for efficient power distribution and billing in AC systems." },
        { t: "RMS and peak values", e: "The RMS (root mean square) value of an alternating current or voltage is the value of steady DC that would produce the same heating effect (power dissipation) in a resistor as the actual alternating quantity does over a full cycle. For a sinusoidal current i = i0*sin(wt), since the mean of sin^2(wt) over a cycle is 1/2, the RMS value is Irms = i0/sqrt(2) ≈ 0.707*i0, and similarly Vrms = v0/sqrt(2). The peak (maximum) value i0 or v0 is simply the amplitude of the sinusoidal waveform, reached once per cycle, which is larger than the RMS value by a factor of sqrt(2). RMS values are used in practice (and on all AC equipment ratings, meters, and mains voltage specifications like 230 V) because they directly relate to power dissipation and heating effects, unlike instantaneous or peak values which vary continuously. This distinction is crucial for correctly calculating power, P = Vrms*Irms*cos(phi), and designing components that can withstand peak voltages and currents during operation." }
      ],
      core: "LCR impedance and resonance frequency derivation; power factor and RMS numericals.",
      prep: "Memorize XL = wL, XC = 1/wC, Z = sqrt(R^2 + (XL-XC)^2) and resonance f = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(LC)); practice impedance and power problems.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A series LCR circuit has L = 2 H and C = 8 uF. Find the resonant frequency.", a: "Resonant frequency f = 1/(2*pi*sqrt(LC)); LC = 2 x 8e-6 = 16e-6; sqrt(LC) = 4e-3; f = 1/(2*pi x 4e-3) = 1/(0.02513) = 39.8 Hz; Final answer: approximately 39.8 Hz." },
        { q: "Define RMS value of alternating current and derive its relation to the peak value.", a: "RMS value is the value of steady DC current that produces the same heating effect as the AC in the same resistance over a cycle; For i = i0*sin(wt), heat over a cycle is proportional to the mean of i^2; Mean of sin^2(wt) over a cycle = 1/2, so mean of i^2 = i0^2/2; Therefore Irms = sqrt(i0^2/2) = i0/sqrt(2) = 0.707*i0; Final relation: Irms = i0/sqrt(2)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Dual Nature of Radiation and Matter",
      wt: "~4 marks",
      explain: "This chapter establishes that radiation and matter both show wave and particle nature. The photoelectric effect demonstrates the particle (photon) nature of light, explained by Einstein's equation. De Broglie's hypothesis assigns wavelength to moving particles.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Photoelectric effect and laws", e: "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light of sufficiently high frequency falls on it, discovered experimentally by Hertz and explained theoretically by Einstein. Experimental laws observed include that photoelectric emission is instantaneous (no time lag), occurs only above a threshold frequency characteristic of the metal, that photocurrent (number of electrons emitted) is proportional to light intensity, but the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons depends only on frequency and not on intensity. These observations could not be explained by the classical wave theory of light, which predicted a time lag and intensity-dependent kinetic energy, motivating a new particle picture. This effect is studied using a photocell where light falls on a photosensitive cathode and electrons are collected at an anode, with current measured as a function of applied voltage, frequency and intensity. The photoelectric effect provided the first strong evidence for the quantum (particle) nature of light." },
        { t: "Einstein's photoelectric equation", e: "Einstein explained the photoelectric effect by proposing that light consists of discrete energy packets called photons, each carrying energy E = hf, where h is Planck's constant and f is the frequency of light. When a photon strikes a metal surface, it is absorbed completely by a single electron, which uses part of this energy (equal to the work function phi) to escape the metal surface, and the rest appears as the electron's kinetic energy. This gives Einstein's photoelectric equation, hf = phi + KEmax, or KEmax = hf - phi, showing maximum kinetic energy increases linearly with frequency above the threshold. This equation correctly predicts the instantaneous nature of emission (since a single photon-electron interaction doesn't need time to accumulate energy), the existence of a threshold frequency (below which hf < phi and no emission occurs), and the independence of KEmax from intensity (since intensity just changes photon number, not individual photon energy). This explanation earned Einstein the Nobel Prize and firmly established the particle (quantum) nature of electromagnetic radiation." },
        { t: "Work function and threshold frequency", e: "Work function phi is the minimum energy required to just free an electron from the surface of a metal, overcoming the attractive forces holding it in the material; it is a characteristic property of each metal, typically measured in electron volts (eV). Threshold frequency f0 is the minimum frequency of incident light that can cause photoelectric emission from a given metal, related to work function by phi = h*f0, below which no electrons are emitted regardless of light intensity. Metals with low work function (like alkali metals: caesium, potassium) have low threshold frequencies and can emit electrons even with visible light, while metals with higher work function require ultraviolet light. The corresponding threshold wavelength is lambda0 = hc/phi, the maximum wavelength of light able to cause emission for a given metal. These concepts explain why certain metals are chosen for photocells and photomultiplier tubes depending on the wavelength range of light to be detected." },
        { t: "Stopping potential", e: "Stopping potential V0 is the minimum negative (retarding) potential applied to the collector electrode in a photocell that is just sufficient to stop even the most energetic photoelectrons from reaching it, reducing photocurrent to zero. It is related to the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons by e*V0 = KEmax, where e is the electron charge, so V0 = (hf - phi)/e, showing stopping potential increases linearly with frequency of incident light. Importantly, stopping potential is independent of the intensity of light, since intensity only affects the number of photoelectrons (and hence photocurrent magnitude) but not their individual maximum energy, confirming the quantum nature of the effect. Plotting stopping potential V0 against frequency f gives a straight line whose slope equals h/e, providing an experimental method to determine Planck's constant, and whose intercept gives the work function. This measurement technique, pioneered by Millikan, provided strong quantitative confirmation of Einstein's photoelectric equation." },
        { t: "De Broglie wavelength", e: "De Broglie's hypothesis proposed that just as light exhibits both wave and particle nature, all moving matter particles also possess an associated wave character, with wavelength given by lambda = h/p = h/(mv), where p is the particle's momentum. This wavelength is inversely proportional to momentum, meaning massive or fast-moving objects (like a cricket ball) have immeasurably small wavelengths, while light particles like electrons moving at typical speeds have wavelengths comparable to atomic dimensions and X-rays, making wave effects observable. The hypothesis extended the idea of wave-particle duality universally to matter, unifying the description of radiation and matter under a single conceptual framework. De Broglie waves are not physical waves of a medium but represent probability amplitude waves in quantum mechanics, later formalized in Schrodinger's wave equation. This concept is essential for understanding electron diffraction, electron microscopy, and the quantum mechanical model of the atom where electrons are treated as having wave properties." },
        { t: "Davisson-Germer experiment", e: "The Davisson-Germer experiment provided direct experimental confirmation of de Broglie's hypothesis by demonstrating diffraction of electrons scattered from a nickel crystal, analogous to X-ray diffraction from crystal lattices. In this experiment, a beam of electrons accelerated through a known potential difference was directed at a nickel crystal, and the intensity of scattered electrons was measured at various angles, revealing a pronounced peak at a specific angle, indicating constructive interference (diffraction). The electron wavelength calculated from the diffraction angle using Bragg's law matched closely with the de Broglie wavelength lambda = h/p calculated from the electron's momentum (determined by the accelerating voltage), confirming the wave nature of matter quantitatively. This result was a landmark in establishing quantum mechanics, as it showed that particles like electrons genuinely exhibit wave behaviour, not just as a mathematical convenience but as a physically observable phenomenon. The experiment's success led to the development of electron diffraction techniques and electron microscopes, widely used today in materials science and biology." }
      ],
      core: "Einstein's photoelectric equation numericals, de Broglie wavelength calculations.",
      prep: "Learn E = hf = phi + KEmax and lambda = h/p = h/(mv); practice stopping potential and de Broglie wavelength numericals.",
      questions: [
        { q: "Light of wavelength 400 nm falls on a metal of work function 2 eV. Find the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons. (h = 6.63e-34 Js, c = 3e8 m/s, 1 eV = 1.6e-19 J).", a: "Photon energy E = hc/lambda = (6.63e-34 x 3e8)/(400e-9) = (1.989e-25)/(4e-7) = 4.97e-19 J; In eV = 4.97e-19/1.6e-19 = 3.11 eV; KEmax = E - phi = 3.11 - 2 = 1.11 eV; Final answer: 1.11 eV (approximately 1.78e-19 J)." },
        { q: "State de Broglie's hypothesis and calculate the wavelength of an electron moving at 1e7 m/s. (mass = 9.1e-31 kg, h = 6.63e-34 Js).", a: "De Broglie hypothesis: every moving particle has an associated wave of wavelength lambda = h/p = h/(mv); Substitute h = 6.63e-34, m = 9.1e-31, v = 1e7; p = 9.1e-31 x 1e7 = 9.1e-24; lambda = 6.63e-34/9.1e-24 = 7.28e-11 m; Final answer: 7.28e-11 m (0.0728 nm)." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Structure of Atoms and Nuclei",
      wt: "~5 marks",
      explain: "This chapter describes atomic structure via Bohr's model and nuclear properties including radioactivity. Bohr's model explains hydrogen spectral lines through quantized orbits. Nuclear binding energy, mass defect and radioactive decay laws are central.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Bohr's atomic model and postulates", e: "Bohr's atomic model was proposed to overcome the limitations of Rutherford's model, which could not explain atomic stability or discrete spectral lines. Bohr's first postulate states that electrons revolve around the nucleus only in certain allowed circular orbits, called stationary states, without radiating energy, overcoming the classical prediction that an accelerating charge should continuously radiate and spiral into the nucleus. The second postulate quantizes angular momentum as L = n*h/(2*pi), where n is a positive integer (principal quantum number), restricting electrons to discrete orbits with specific radii and energies. The third postulate states that an electron can jump between orbits by absorbing or emitting a photon of energy exactly equal to the energy difference between the two orbits, E = hf = E2 - E1, explaining the origin of discrete spectral lines. These postulates successfully explained the hydrogen atom's stability and its line spectrum, though the model was later superseded by full quantum mechanics for complex atoms." },
        { t: "Energy levels and spectral series of hydrogen", e: "Using Bohr's postulates, the energy of an electron in the nth orbit of hydrogen is derived as En = -13.6/n^2 eV, showing energy levels are discrete, negative (bound states), and become less negative (closer to zero) as n increases, approaching ionization at n = infinity. When an electron transitions from a higher energy level n2 to a lower level n1, it emits a photon of energy equal to the difference, giving the spectral line formula 1/lambda = R(1/n1^2 - 1/n2^2), where R is the Rydberg constant. Transitions ending at n1 = 1 produce the Lyman series (ultraviolet), at n1 = 2 produce the Balmer series (visible), and at n1 = 3 produce the Paschen series (infrared), each series showing a characteristic pattern of spectral lines converging at a series limit. This quantitative agreement between Bohr's theoretical formula and the experimentally observed hydrogen spectrum was one of the most convincing early successes of quantum theory. These spectral series are used in astrophysics to identify elements in distant stars from their characteristic emission or absorption lines." },
        { t: "Nuclear size, density and mass defect", e: "The nucleus occupies an extremely small volume at the centre of an atom, with radius given empirically by R = R0*A^(1/3), where R0 ≈ 1.2 fm and A is the mass number, showing nuclear volume is proportional to the number of nucleons, implying nearly constant nuclear density (≈ 2.3 x 10^17 kg/m^3) for all nuclei, independent of A. Mass defect is the difference between the sum of the masses of individual free nucleons (protons and neutrons) and the actual measured mass of the bound nucleus, delta_m = (Zmp + Nmn) - M_nucleus, arising because some mass is converted into binding energy when nucleons combine. This mass defect, though tiny in kilograms, corresponds to an enormous energy via Einstein's relation E = delta_m*c^2, which is released when the nucleus forms and must be supplied to split it apart again. These concepts explain why nuclei are extraordinarily dense and stable, and form the basis for calculating the binding energy that holds nucleons together against the repulsive Coulomb force between protons." },
        { t: "Binding energy per nucleon", e: "Binding energy of a nucleus is the energy equivalent of its mass defect, BE = delta_m*c^2, representing the energy required to completely separate a nucleus into its individual free protons and neutrons. Binding energy per nucleon, BE/A, is a more useful measure of nuclear stability since it normalizes for different nuclear sizes; plotting BE/A against mass number A gives a curve that rises steeply for light nuclei, peaks around A = 56 (iron, the most stable nucleus), and gradually decreases for heavier nuclei. This curve explains both nuclear fission, where heavy nuclei (low BE/A) split into more stable medium nuclei (higher BE/A) releasing energy, and nuclear fusion, where light nuclei (low BE/A) combine into more stable medium nuclei releasing even greater energy per nucleon. The sharp rise at low A also explains why fusion of light elements (like hydrogen into helium, as in stars) releases enormous amounts of energy. Understanding this binding energy curve is essential to explaining the energy released in nuclear reactors (fission) and in stellar energy generation and hydrogen bombs (fusion)." },
        { t: "Radioactive decay law and half life", e: "The radioactive decay law states that the rate of disintegration of radioactive nuclei at any instant is directly proportional to the number of undecayed nuclei present at that instant, -dN/dt = lambda*N, where lambda is the decay constant characteristic of the radioactive species. Integrating this gives the exponential decay formula N = N0*e^(-lambda*t), showing the number of undecayed nuclei decreases exponentially with time, never reaching exactly zero but approaching it asymptotically. Half-life T(1/2) is the time required for half of the original radioactive nuclei to decay, related to the decay constant by T(1/2) = 0.693/lambda, and after n half-lives, the fraction remaining is (1/2)^n. The decay process is random and spontaneous at the level of individual nuclei but statistically predictable for large numbers, independent of external conditions like temperature or pressure. Half-life measurements are used in radiometric dating (e.g., carbon-14 dating of archaeological samples) and in medical applications for selecting appropriate radioactive isotopes for diagnosis or treatment." },
        { t: "Alpha, beta and gamma decay", e: "Alpha decay occurs when an unstable heavy nucleus emits an alpha particle (two protons and two neutrons, same as a helium nucleus), reducing its mass number by 4 and atomic number by 2, typically occurring in very heavy nuclei to reduce excess binding energy imbalance, e.g., Uranium-238 decaying to Thorium-234. Beta decay occurs when a neutron inside the nucleus converts into a proton while emitting an electron (beta-minus particle) and an antineutrino, increasing atomic number by 1 while mass number stays the same, occurring in nuclei with excess neutrons relative to the stable ratio. Gamma decay involves the emission of a high-energy photon (gamma ray) from a nucleus that is left in an excited energy state after alpha or beta decay, allowing the nucleus to lose excess energy and settle into its ground state without changing its mass number or atomic number. These three decay modes are governed by the fundamental forces and conservation laws (charge, mass-energy, momentum) and together explain how unstable nuclei transform to reach more stable configurations. Understanding these decay types is essential for nuclear medicine, radiation shielding design, and interpreting radioactive decay chains found in nature." }
      ],
      core: "Bohr radius/energy derivation, hydrogen spectral lines, radioactive decay and half life numericals.",
      prep: "Learn En = -13.6/n^2 eV, 1/lambda = R(1/n1^2 - 1/n2^2), and N = N0*e^(-lambda*t); practice half life and binding energy numericals.",
      questions: [
        { q: "A radioactive sample has half life 5 years. What fraction remains after 15 years?", a: "Number of half lives n = 15/5 = 3; Fraction remaining = (1/2)^n = (1/2)^3 = 1/8 = 0.125; Final answer: 1/8 (12.5%) of the sample remains." },
        { q: "Using Bohr's model, find the energy of the electron in the second orbit of hydrogen and the energy of a photon emitted when it falls to the ground state.", a: "Energy of nth orbit En = -13.6/n^2 eV; For n = 2, E2 = -13.6/4 = -3.4 eV; For n = 1, E1 = -13.6 eV; Energy of emitted photon = E2 - E1 = -3.4 - (-13.6) = 10.2 eV; Final answer: E2 = -3.4 eV, photon energy = 10.2 eV." }
      ]
    },
    {
      t: "Semiconductor Devices",
      wt: "~4 marks",
      explain: "This chapter covers semiconductor physics and devices like diodes and transistors. The key idea is the p-n junction and how it conducts asymmetrically, enabling rectification. Transistors as amplifiers and switches, plus logic gates, are introduced.",
      topics: [
        { t: "Intrinsic and extrinsic semiconductors", e: "Intrinsic semiconductors are pure elemental crystals (like silicon or germanium) where conduction occurs due to thermally generated electron-hole pairs, with equal numbers of free electrons and holes, giving relatively poor conductivity at room temperature that increases with temperature. Extrinsic semiconductors are created by doping an intrinsic semiconductor with a small controlled amount of impurity atoms to drastically increase conductivity and control the dominant charge carrier type. Doping with pentavalent impurities (like phosphorus or arsenic) produces n-type semiconductors, where extra electrons are the majority carriers and holes are minority carriers. Doping with trivalent impurities (like boron or aluminium) produces p-type semiconductors, where holes are the majority carriers and electrons are minority carriers. This controlled doping process is the foundation of all modern semiconductor device fabrication, allowing precise engineering of electrical properties for diodes, transistors and integrated circuits." },
        { t: "p-n junction and biasing", e: "A p-n junction is formed when a p-type and n-type semiconductor are joined, creating a thin depletion region at the junction due to diffusion of electrons and holes across the boundary, which establishes an internal potential barrier (barrier potential, around 0.7 V for silicon) opposing further diffusion. In forward bias, the p-side is connected to the positive terminal and n-side to negative, which reduces the barrier potential, narrows the depletion region, and allows significant current to flow as majority carriers are pushed across the junction. In reverse bias, the p-side is connected to negative and n-side to positive, which increases the barrier potential, widens the depletion region, and allows only a very small reverse saturation current (due to minority carriers) to flow. This asymmetric conduction behaviour, easily conducting in forward bias but blocking in reverse bias, is the fundamental property exploited in diode applications like rectification. The current-voltage (I-V) characteristic curve of a p-n junction diode clearly shows this nonlinear asymmetric behaviour, distinguishing it from a simple resistor." },
        { t: "Rectifiers (half and full wave)", e: "A rectifier converts alternating current (AC) into direct current (DC) by exploiting the one-way conduction property of a p-n junction diode. In a half-wave rectifier, a single diode allows current to flow only during one half (say positive) of each AC cycle when it is forward biased, blocking the other half when reverse biased, so the output is a series of pulses occurring once per input cycle, with relatively low efficiency and significant ripple. In a full-wave rectifier, either using a centre-tapped transformer with two diodes or a bridge arrangement of four diodes, current flows through the load during both halves of the AC cycle (using different diode paths), producing twice as many output pulses per cycle and much smoother, more efficient DC output. The output ripple in both cases is normally smoothed further using a capacitor filter to approximate pure DC for powering electronic circuits. Rectifiers are the first essential stage in most DC power supplies, converting mains AC into usable DC for electronic devices and battery chargers." },
        { t: "Zener diode and voltage regulation", e: "A Zener diode is a specially designed p-n junction diode intended to operate in the reverse breakdown region, where at a specific reverse voltage (the Zener voltage) the current increases sharply while the voltage across the diode remains nearly constant over a wide range of currents. This characteristic occurs due to the Zener effect (quantum tunnelling of electrons across a narrow depletion region at high field) or avalanche effect (carrier multiplication) depending on the doping level and voltage range. Because the voltage across a Zener diode stays essentially constant despite changes in current through it (within its rated power limits), it is used as a voltage regulator by connecting it in reverse bias in parallel with the load, maintaining a stable output voltage even when the input voltage or load current fluctuates. A series resistor is used to limit current through the Zener diode and absorb excess voltage from the unregulated source. Zener diodes are widely used in power supply circuits to provide stable reference voltages and protect sensitive electronic components from voltage spikes." },
        { t: "Transistor action and configurations", e: "A transistor is a three-terminal semiconductor device (emitter, base, collector) formed by two p-n junctions, typically used as an n-p-n or p-n-p structure, where the thin, lightly doped base region controls a much larger current flowing between emitter and collector. Transistor action relies on the emitter-base junction being forward biased (injecting many majority carriers into the thin base) and the collector-base junction being reverse biased (sweeping these carriers across to the collector), so a small base current controls a much larger collector current, giving current amplification characterized by beta = Ic/Ib, typically 50-200 or more. The three common configurations are common base, common emitter and common collector, named by which terminal is common to both input and output circuits, each offering different combinations of current gain, voltage gain and impedance matching properties, with common emitter being most widely used for amplification due to its favourable combination of current and voltage gain. Transistors can operate as amplifiers (in the linear region) to boost small signals, or as switches (saturation/cutoff regions) in digital circuits, forming the building block of nearly all modern electronics. Understanding transistor characteristics (input/output curves) is essential for designing amplifier circuits with correct biasing for distortion-free operation." },
        { t: "Logic gates", e: "Logic gates are the basic building blocks of digital electronic circuits that perform fundamental Boolean logic operations on one or more binary inputs (0 or 1, representing low/high voltage) to produce a single binary output, typically implemented using diodes and transistors. The basic gates are: AND gate (output is 1 only if all inputs are 1), OR gate (output is 1 if any input is 1), and NOT gate (inverts the single input, 0 becomes 1 and vice versa); these are combined to form universal gates NAND (NOT-AND) and NOR (NOT-OR), from which any logic function can be constructed. Each gate's behaviour is completely described by its truth table, listing the output for every possible combination of inputs, which is essential for designing and verifying digital circuits. These gates are physically realized using transistor circuits operating in cutoff (output high, representing 1) and saturation (output low, representing 0) states, exploiting the switching property of transistors. Logic gates form the foundation of digital electronics, enabling the construction of adders, memory circuits, microprocessors and all computational hardware." }
      ],
      core: "p-n junction and rectifier working; transistor as amplifier; logic gate truth tables.",
      prep: "Understand forward/reverse bias characteristics and rectifier circuits; memorize logic gate symbols and truth tables; practice transistor current relations.",
      questions: [
        { q: "In a transistor the emitter current is 10 mA and the base current is 0.1 mA. Find the current gain beta and alpha.", a: "Collector current Ic = Ie - Ib = 10 - 0.1 = 9.9 mA; beta = Ic/Ib = 9.9/0.1 = 99; alpha = Ic/Ie = 9.9/10 = 0.99; Check: beta = alpha/(1-alpha) = 0.99/0.01 = 99; Final answer: beta = 99, alpha = 0.99." },
        { q: "Explain the working of a p-n junction diode as a half wave rectifier.", a: "The AC input is applied across the diode in series with a load resistor; During the positive half cycle the diode is forward biased and conducts, so current flows through the load producing output voltage; During the negative half cycle the diode is reverse biased and does not conduct, giving no output; Thus only one half of each AC cycle appears across the load, converting AC to pulsating DC; This is half wave rectification with output frequency equal to input frequency." }
      ]
    }
  ]
};